WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.000
Predictive modeling is a process of modeling&nbsp;
historical data for predicting future events.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.600
For example, we want to use electronic health&nbsp;
record that we have available to view a model of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.200
heart failure. So that we can predict patients who&nbsp;
are at risk of developing heart failure sooner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.400
Predictive modeling is not a single&nbsp;
algorithm, but a computational pipeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.280
that involves multiple steps. The key&nbsp;
goal we want to answer in this lesson is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.600
how do we develop a good predictive model&nbsp;
using electronic health record quickly.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.520
First, we decide the prediction target, for&nbsp;
example, whether a patient will develop heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.720
failure in the next few years. Second, we&nbsp;
construct the cohort of relevant patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.920
for the study. Third, we define all the&nbsp;
potentially relevant features for the study.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.880
Fourth, we select which features are&nbsp;
actually relevant for predicting the target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.560
Fifth, we compute the predictive model, and&nbsp;
sixth, we evaluate the predictive model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.160
Then we iterate this process several times&nbsp;
until we are satisfied with the resulting model.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.640
Prediction Target
There are often many targets that an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.880
investigator wants to predict using the data they&nbsp;
have. However, only a subset of them are possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.920
So, we should choose the prediction target&nbsp;
that addresses the primary question that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.840
both interesting to the investigator and&nbsp;
possible to be answered using the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.560
In this lecture, let’s focus on&nbsp;
predicting the onset of heart failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.280
which is an interesting and&nbsp;
potentially possible target.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.200
Motivations for Early Detection
If we want to develop a predictive model for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.280
heart failure, what are the motivations for early&nbsp;
detection of heart failure? First, heart failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:51.680
is a complex disease. There is no widely accepted&nbsp;
characterization and definition of heart failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.920
Probably because the complexity of the&nbsp;
syndrome. It has many potential ideologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:04.160
diverse clinical features, and numerous clinical&nbsp;
subsets. If we can detect heart failure earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.560
we can potentially reduce the cost of&nbsp;
hospitalization associated with heart failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.440
We can also potentially introduce new&nbsp;
early intervention to try to slow down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.840
the progression of heart failure, improve&nbsp;
the quality of life, and reduce mortality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:26.960
In the long term we can improve existing&nbsp;
clinical guidelines for heart failure prevention.

